1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,400
 Alright, having talked generally about how the arrangement of the constituents in a composite

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:08,400
 affect the material properties, we're now going to talk specifically about how mechanical

3
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:12,640
 properties evolve in a composite due to these arrangements. Because this topic gets very,

4
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:16,480
 very complicated, very, very quickly as you start to model the real world,

5
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:19,840
 we're going to be pretty subdued in terms of the approach that we take.

6
00:00:19,840 --> 00:00:23,120
 We're only going to talk about two constituent components loaded

7
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:28,160
 actually in their principal directions. In other words, loaded in series or in parallel.

8
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:31,840
 At the end of this video, we'll talk briefly about extensions of this to other geometries,

9
00:00:31,840 --> 00:00:36,320
 but we will talk more about that in context as we meet some of those specific composites

10
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:41,120
 later in the week. Before we start a note on syntax on terminology, in this course, we're using

11
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:46,560
 a particular convention in our definition of series and parallel, kind of the intuitive definition

12
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:51,440
 that two things lined up in series are aligned end to end and two things lined up in parallel

13
00:00:51,440 --> 00:00:57,120
 or aligned side by side. This convention isn't used universally. For example, in end 2440,

14
00:00:57,120 --> 00:01:00,960
 there is a different convention that's used. Neither of these approaches is right or wrong.

15
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:04,560
 Convention is just that. It's like choosing a direction for your coordinate system,

16
00:01:04,560 --> 00:01:07,840
 but you should be aware that different people and different courses might have a different

17
00:01:07,840 --> 00:01:12,560
 perspective on what exactly you call series and what exactly you call parallel. But really,

18
00:01:12,560 --> 00:01:16,160
 this is just a naming convention. We'll kick off our discussion on mechanical properties by

19
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:20,400
 starting with the elasticity of composites. The same way we do with every other material.

20
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:25,920
 Here, like every other material we've met this semester, a composite acts essentially like a spring,

21
00:01:25,920 --> 00:01:30,080
 as it's loaded up. It deforms elastically by some amount, stretching the atomic bonds inside the

22
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:35,200
 material, which provide a restoring force. But unlike many of the materials we've met so far in

23
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:40,800
 this course, which were relatively isotropic, composites in general are very anti-sotropy.

24
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:45,040
 They have very different behavior depending on whether the components are aligned in series

25
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:49,600
 or in parallel. If components in a composite are aligned in series, the same way you might have,

26
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:55,200
 if you had discrete layers in a fiber reinforced composite that's being loaded kind of across these

27
00:01:55,680 --> 00:02:00,000
 or a sandwich structure that's being loaded across its layers, then the effective elastic

28
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,880
 behavior of this composite is going to be very much like two springs connected back to back.

29
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:08,880
 In this arrangement, there are two key constraints on the elastic behavior. First of all,

30
00:02:08,880 --> 00:02:13,520
 the total deformation seen by the composite, the effective deformation of the composite,

31
00:02:13,520 --> 00:02:19,280
 is a sum of the deformation of the individual parts. And second of all, any applied load needs

32
00:02:19,280 --> 00:02:24,240
 to be carried in its entirety by every constituent of the composite. For a load to reach a

33
00:02:24,240 --> 00:02:28,880
 or restraint, it has to pass through every single constituent in turn. This gives rise to two

34
00:02:28,880 --> 00:02:34,160
 pretty important expressions, one for the effective stiffness of a series comprised composite,

35
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:39,040
 given the stiffnesses of each of the constituents, and one that describes how each of the constituents

36
00:02:39,040 --> 00:02:43,680
 to form to make up the total deformation. The derivation for these expressions is linked on the textbook

37
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:46,960
 page for this video. I'm not going to go through it now because I think the vast majority of

38
00:02:46,960 --> 00:02:50,400
 people aren't interested in hearing a derivation. But it's up there on the textbook page,

39
00:02:50,400 --> 00:02:53,760
 you can check it out if you're interested. There are a couple of interesting observations about

40
00:02:53,760 --> 00:02:58,400
 series aligned composites that we can draw from these expressions. First of all, in a series arranged

41
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:03,440
 composite, the effective stiffness depends on the product of the stiffnesses of the constituents.

42
00:03:03,440 --> 00:03:07,680
 This means that if you have one constituent which is very, very compliant, the entire composite

43
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:12,400
 is going to suffer. Second of all, there's a hard upper bound on the effective stiffness of the

44
00:03:12,400 --> 00:03:17,040
 composite. As the stiffness of one constituent goes to infinity as it essentially becomes rigid,

45
00:03:17,040 --> 00:03:21,600
 the effective stiffness of the composite tends towards that of the other constituent. In other

46
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:26,080
 words, if you had a series aligned composite that consisted of a nearly rigid material and a very

47
00:03:26,080 --> 00:03:31,200
 compliant material, the effective stiffness would be very close to that of the very compliant material.

48
00:03:31,200 --> 00:03:35,760
 Finally, and probably very intuitively, the deformation contributed by each constituent is a

49
00:03:35,760 --> 00:03:40,400
 function of its stiffness. In other words, the stiffer constituents deform less and the more

50
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:45,280
 compliant constituents deform more. Now, there are analogies but very different expressions for

51
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:49,520
 a parallel arranged composite. A real world example of a parallel arranged composite would be

52
00:03:49,520 --> 00:03:54,080
 something like a fiber reinforced polymer that's loaded in the direction of its layers,

53
00:03:54,080 --> 00:03:58,640
 as opposed to through them. The elastic behavior of such a composite is very much like having two

54
00:03:58,640 --> 00:04:03,040
 springs in parallel. And as was the case with series aligned composites, there are two key

55
00:04:03,040 --> 00:04:07,520
 upshots, two key constraints on the elastic behavior that arise from this arrangement.

56
00:04:07,520 --> 00:04:11,680
 First of all, parallel arranged composites are constrained such that the deformation seen by

57
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:16,080
 each constituent is equal. Because the two constituents are arranged in parallel, they have to

58
00:04:16,160 --> 00:04:21,360
 deform by the same amount. Second of all, because they're arranged in parallel, not every constituent

59
00:04:21,360 --> 00:04:26,320
 needs to bare the entire load. In fact, each constituent will instead contribute some proportion

60
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:30,880
 of the total load will carry some proportion of the total load. These upshots give rise to

61
00:04:30,880 --> 00:04:35,600
 expressions for the effective stiffness of a parallel arranged composite and for the proportion

62
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:40,240
 of the load that's carried by each constituent in a parallel arranged composite. And as was the

63
00:04:40,240 --> 00:04:43,760
 case with series arranged composites, there are some interesting conclusions that we can draw from

64
00:04:43,760 --> 00:04:47,920
 these expressions. First of all, and probably most importantly, the effective stiffness of a

65
00:04:47,920 --> 00:04:52,240
 parallel arranged composite doesn't depend on a product of the constituent's differences.

66
00:04:52,240 --> 00:04:57,120
 It only depends on a simple sum. This means that a very, very compliant constituent in the stack

67
00:04:57,120 --> 00:05:02,000
 up doesn't affect the effective stiffness of the composite as much. And it also means that the

68
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:06,160
 effective stiffness isn't bound by whatever the lowest of the constituent's differences are.

69
00:05:06,160 --> 00:05:11,840
 The stiffness can go to infinity as either of the constituents do. Finally, and I hope intuitively,

70
00:05:11,840 --> 00:05:16,080
 the proportion of load carried by each constituent is a function of its stiffness with

71
00:05:16,080 --> 00:05:20,640
 the stiffer constituents carrying more load. Now, that was a lot of information to take in in

72
00:05:20,640 --> 00:05:25,440
 not very much time, so let's recap and bring these things together now. In a series aligned

73
00:05:25,440 --> 00:05:30,320
 composite, the load has to be carried equally and completely by every member. And every

74
00:05:30,320 --> 00:05:34,720
 member will deform by potentially a different amount to contribute to the total defamation

75
00:05:34,720 --> 00:05:39,600
 seen by the part. Consequently, the effective stiffness of a series aligned composite is very

76
00:05:39,680 --> 00:05:44,640
 sensitive to both constituent's differences. It's sensitive to a product of these. And series

77
00:05:44,640 --> 00:05:49,040
 aligned composites for that reason tend to be a little bit more compliant, a little bit less stiff

78
00:05:49,040 --> 00:05:55,200
 than their parallel counterparts. Speaking of parallel aligned composites, here, the load is shared

79
00:05:55,200 --> 00:06:00,320
 amongst all of the constituents and doesn't need to be carried in whole by each one. Instead,

80
00:06:00,320 --> 00:06:05,440
 each constituent contributes some proportion of the total load bearing capacity of the composite.

81
00:06:05,520 --> 00:06:10,320
 That being said, each constituent is subject to the same deformation. They all need to stretch

82
00:06:10,320 --> 00:06:14,960
 or compress by the same amount. In a parallel aligned composite, the effective stiffness is a

83
00:06:14,960 --> 00:06:19,760
 simple sum of the constituent's differences. And this means that it isn't bound by either of them.

84
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:24,000
 And it also means that if you have a very compliant constituent, it's not going to penalize the

85
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:28,400
 effective stiffness as much as a series aligned one. All right, up until this point, I've been

86
00:06:28,400 --> 00:06:33,040
 talking about stiffness, but the time has come for us to tie this near to material properties.

87
00:06:33,040 --> 00:06:36,800
 It turns out that irrespective of whether you're talking about an axial loading or a

88
00:06:36,800 --> 00:06:42,320
 torsional loading or a bending loading, the stiffness of an engineering material is always

89
00:06:42,320 --> 00:06:47,120
 related to young's modulus. And while the specific expressions will differ for these different

90
00:06:47,120 --> 00:06:52,960
 use cases, the effective young's modulus of a component is related to the effective stiffness

91
00:06:52,960 --> 00:06:58,240
 of that component. And this relationship is essentially analogous to the ones we've just talked

92
00:06:58,240 --> 00:07:03,280
 about for stiffness. If, for example, we consider a series arranged composite and again,

93
00:07:03,280 --> 00:07:08,560
 only talk about axial loadings and further make an assumption that all of the constituents of this

94
00:07:08,560 --> 00:07:13,200
 composite have the same cross-sectional area, we get this expression where the effective young's

95
00:07:13,200 --> 00:07:18,320
 modulus, just like the effective stiffness, depends on a product of the constituent moduli.

96
00:07:18,320 --> 00:07:22,880
 If we have a parallel arranged composite and assume that each constituent has the same length,

97
00:07:22,880 --> 00:07:26,720
 then we get this expression where the effective young's modulus is essentially

98
00:07:26,720 --> 00:07:31,600
 weighted some of the constituent moduli. The derivations for both of these expressions are up

99
00:07:31,600 --> 00:07:35,520
 on the textbook page for this video. They're a little bit more complicated than the stiffness

100
00:07:35,520 --> 00:07:39,840
 ones, but not hugely. But I hope you can see by looking at these expressions that the same

101
00:07:39,840 --> 00:07:44,720
 takeaways as applied to stiffness apply to young's modulus. In a series arranged

102
00:07:44,720 --> 00:07:48,560
 composite, the introduction of a compliant material, one with a low young's modulus,

103
00:07:48,560 --> 00:07:53,760
 is going to severely drag down the effective modulus of the composite. In a parallel arranged

104
00:07:53,760 --> 00:07:57,600
 composite, not nearly as much. To put some numbers on this, to give you an example,

105
00:07:57,600 --> 00:08:02,160
 let's look at both cases, let's say I have a 50-50 mixture of a really stiff material like

106
00:08:02,160 --> 00:08:07,360
 alumina and a really, really compliant material like polyethylene. Illumina has a young's modulus

107
00:08:07,360 --> 00:08:12,720
 in the order of 330 gigapascals. Polyethylene has a young's modulus in the order of three quarters

108
00:08:12,720 --> 00:08:17,760
 of a gigapascal. In a series arranged, composite, the effective modulus depends on a product of these

109
00:08:17,760 --> 00:08:23,280
 things and consequently ends up being only one and a half gigapascals, basically the same as that

110
00:08:23,280 --> 00:08:28,640
 of polyethylene. In a parallel arranged composite, the effective modulus depends on a weighted

111
00:08:28,640 --> 00:08:33,600
 sum of these and consequently looks more like an average, about halfway between polyethylene

112
00:08:33,600 --> 00:08:38,560
 and alumina. And I hope this agrees with your intuitive understanding. If you need to pass a load

113
00:08:38,560 --> 00:08:42,400
 through all of the components, then you would imagine that you're essentially limited by

114
00:08:42,400 --> 00:08:46,960
 whichever of those is the most compliant. And if you can share a load across a bunch of different

115
00:08:46,960 --> 00:08:52,160
 components, then essentially the only penalty you suffer by introduction of a more compliant one

116
00:08:52,160 --> 00:08:56,960
 is that you have less cross-sectional area to fill with the stiffer one. Now for situations that

117
00:08:56,960 --> 00:09:01,600
 aren't perfectly series or perfectly parallel say that maybe you do have a laminated composite,

118
00:09:01,600 --> 00:09:05,920
 but you have some load which is oblique to the layers, or if you have a composite that consists of

119
00:09:05,920 --> 00:09:10,880
 one material within another, well then these equations aren't going to strictly apply. That being

120
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:16,880
 said, series and parallel represent the two extremes in terms of orientation of your constituents.

121
00:09:16,880 --> 00:09:21,520
 And any effective stiffness or effective young's modulus is going to be bound by these extremes

122
00:09:21,520 --> 00:09:25,600
 is going to live somewhere between these extremes. We'll talk a little bit more about these

123
00:09:25,600 --> 00:09:30,560
 in context over the next few videos, but I will note that particularly as you start to move towards

124
00:09:30,560 --> 00:09:36,400
 more complicated geometries and more complicated loading scenarios, this problem very quickly becomes

125
00:09:36,400 --> 00:09:41,280
 intractable to do by hand. In fact, both properties of engineering composites, whether those be

126
00:09:41,280 --> 00:09:46,160
 mechanical or even non-mechanical, are very often simulated. There are a bunch of different

127
00:09:46,160 --> 00:09:49,920
 techniques for doing this, but they're all well and truly outside the scope of this course. So

128
00:09:49,920 --> 00:09:54,160
 I might leave that one there. To finish up this video, I'm going to very briefly talk about

129
00:09:54,160 --> 00:09:58,320
 strength of composites, and it can be a very brief discussion because we've already done

130
00:09:58,320 --> 00:10:03,520
 all the hard yards in our discussion of stiffness. In a series aligned composite because every

131
00:10:03,520 --> 00:10:08,240
 constituent needs to bear the entire load, then the effective strength of your composite is

132
00:10:08,240 --> 00:10:13,200
 essentially the minimum of the strengths of your constituents. Because every constituent has to

133
00:10:13,200 --> 00:10:18,000
 carry the entire load, you can't really benefit from the incredible strength of one constituent

134
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:23,280
 if the other one is going to let you down. For example, in our hypothetical aluminum polyethylene

135
00:10:23,280 --> 00:10:27,440
 composite, you'd never be able to exploit the incredible strength of the aluminum because your

136
00:10:27,440 --> 00:10:33,280
 polyethylene is going to fail at some ridiculously low stress. For this exact reason, many series

137
00:10:33,280 --> 00:10:38,080
 aligned composites often suffer from what we call delamination, where essentially the constituents

138
00:10:38,080 --> 00:10:43,680
 are pulled apart from each other due to a failure of the interconstituent bonding. In a parallel

139
00:10:43,680 --> 00:10:48,160
 arrangement, though your effective strength is a little bit more complicated. Because each constituent

140
00:10:48,160 --> 00:10:53,120
 carries a proportion of the load relative to its stiffness, relative to its young's modulus,

141
00:10:53,120 --> 00:10:57,680
 it can be a little tricky to work out how much load is being carried by each constituent and

142
00:10:57,680 --> 00:11:03,120
 correspondingly which constituent is going to fail first. Essentially, it all comes down to a

143
00:11:03,120 --> 00:11:07,920
 stiffness to strength ratio. A stiffer material will carry more of the load, but if it has a

144
00:11:07,920 --> 00:11:12,960
 sufficiently high strength, it may not fail before the other one does. Essentially, when analyzing

145
00:11:13,280 --> 00:11:17,440
 a composite to fail, you need to very carefully consider the stiffness to strength ratios of the

146
00:11:17,440 --> 00:11:22,000
 constituents. And that'll probably do is for mechanical properties. To make one final point,

147
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:26,240
 I should note that, well, I've made it sound like you can choose between series or parallel

148
00:11:26,240 --> 00:11:30,960
 arrangements in your composite. It's very often the case that these things arise as a function

149
00:11:30,960 --> 00:11:35,120
 of the way they're manufactured. And that you may have no choice in whether a composite is loaded

150
00:11:35,120 --> 00:11:39,760
 in series or parallel at all. Obviously, if you can choose and you can set your composite up

151
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:44,240
 to behave a particular way, then that's great. But very often, when using a composite, you may

152
00:11:44,240 --> 00:11:48,880
 be making the best of a bad situation and subject to the limitations of whatever manufacturing

153
00:11:48,880 --> 00:11:53,040
 technique you'd like to use. We'll talk a little bit more about this and the trade-offs involved

154
00:11:53,040 --> 00:11:56,560
 in doing so next week when we get to manufacturing with composites.
